readings.c1s1:
cious, prudent, cunning (whence the F. surname ''Guiscard''), with suffix ''-ard'', < Icel. ''vizkr'', clever, knowing, sagacious, for *''vitskr'', < ''vita'', know: see ''wit''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''witch''<sup>1</sup>, ult. from the same root, but having no immediate connection with ''wizard''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1†.''' A wise man; a sage.
{{smaller block|Hee that cannot personate the wise-man well among ''wizards'', let him learne to play the foole well amongst dizzards.
{{right|''Chapman'', Masque of Middle Temple and Lincoln’s Inn.}}
<poem>See how from far, upon the eastern road,
The star-led ''wizards'' haste with odours sweet.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Nativity, l. 23.}}}}
'''2.''' A proficient in the occult sciences; an adept in the black art; one supposed to possess supernatural powers, generally from having leagued himself with the Evil One; a sorcerer; an enchanter; a magician; hence, a title occasionally applied to, or assumed by, modern performers of legerdemain; a conjurer; a juggler. See ''witch''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|And the soul that turneth after such as have familiar spirits, and after ''wizards'', . . . I will even set my face against that soul.
{{right|Lev. xx. 6.}}
If by any Accident they do hear of the Thief, all is ascrib’d to the wonderful Cunning of their ''Wissard''.
{{right|Quoted in ''Ashton’s'' Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne,}}
{{right|[I. 121.}}
No ''wizards'' now ply their trade of selling favorable winds to the Norwegian coasters.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Northern Travel, p. 136.}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' Magic; having magical powers; enchanting: as, a ''wizard'' spell.
{{smaller block|<poem>Where Deva spreads her ''wizard'' stream.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Lycidas, l. 55.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''wizardly''' (wiz′ärd-li), ''adv.'' [< ''wizard'' + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Resembling a wizard; characteristic of a wizard. [Rare.]

'''wizardry''' (wiz′ärd-ri), ''n.'' [< ''wizard'' + ''-ry''.] The art or practices of wizards; sorcery.
{{smaller block|''Wizardry'' and dealing with evil spirits.
{{right|''Milman'', Latin Christianity, xi. 9.}}}}

'''wize†.''' An old spelling of ''wise''<sup>1</sup>, ''wise''<sup>2</sup>.

'''wizen'''<sup>1</sup> (wiz′n), ''a.'' [Also ''weazen'', and formerly ''wizzen'', ''wisen''; < ME. *''wisen'', < AS. *''wisen'' = Icel. ''visinn'' = Sw. Dan. ''vissen'', withered, dried up; pp. of a lost verb, AS. as if *''wisan'', dry up. Hence ''wizen''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Hard, dry, and shriveled; withered.
{{smaller block|A gay little ''wizen'' old man, in appearance, from the Eastern climate’s dilapidations upon his youth and health.
{{right|''Mme. D’Arblay'', Diary, Dec., 1791.}}
His shadowy figure and dark ''weazen'' face.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 284.}}
I remember the elder Mathews, a ''wizen'' dark man, with one high shoulder, a distorted mouth, a lame leg, and an irritable manner.
{{right|''E. H. Yates'', Fifty Years of London Life, I. i.}}}}

'''wizen'''<sup>1</sup> (wiz′n), ''v. t.'' and ''i.'' [Also ''weazen'', and formerly ''wizzen'', ''wisen''; < ME. ''wisenen'', < AS. ''wisnian'', also ''forwisnian'' (= Icel. ''visna'' = Sw. ''vissna'' = Dan. ''visne''), become dry, wither, < *''wisen'', dried up, wizen.] To become dry or withered; shrivel; cause to fade; make dry. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|<poem>O ill befa’ your ''wizzen’d'' snout!</poem>
{{right|''Gight’s Lady'' (Child’s Ballads, VIII. 290).}}
<poem>A shoemaker’s lad
With ''wizened'' face in want of soap.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Christmas Eve.}}}}

'''wizen'''<sup>2</sup> (wiz′n), ''n.'' An obsolete or dialectal form of ''weasand''.

readings.c2s2:
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Woad (''Isatis tinctoria'').}}}}
with ''weld''<sup>1</sup>, which has a var. ''wold''.] A cruciferous plant, ''Isatis tinctoria'', formerly much cultivated in Great Britain on account of the blue dye extracted from its pulped and fermented leaves. {{smaller|It is now, however, nearly superseded by indigo, which gives a stronger and finer blue. It is still cultivated in some parts of Europe, and the dye which it furnishes is said to improve the quality and color of indigo when mixed with it in a certain proportion. The ancient Britons are said to have stained their bodies with the dye procured from the woad-plant.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>No mader, welde, or ''wood'' [var. ''wod''] no litestere
Ne knew.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Former Age, l. 17.}}
But now our soile either will not or . . . may not beare either ''wad'' or madder.
{{right|''Harrison'', Descrip. of Britain, [xviii.}}
Admit no difference between ''oade'' and frankincense.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Poetaster, ii. 1.}}}}
'''Wild woad.''' {{smaller|Same as ''weld''<sup>1</sup>.}}

¶
'''woaded''' (wō′ded), ''a.'' [< ''woad'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' Dyed or colored blue with woad.
{{smaller block|<poem>Then the monster, then the man;
Tattoo’d or ''woaded'', winter-clad in skins.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, ii.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''wodness†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''woodness.''

'''woe''' (wō), ''interj.'' [Also ''wo''; Sc. ''wae''; < ME. ''wo'', ''woo'', *''wa'', ''we'', ''waei'', ''wei'', ''wai'', ''wæ'', < AS. ''wā'', interj., sometimes used with dat. case, also in combination ''wā lā'', ''wā lā wā'', also ''wālā wā'', alas! lit. woe! lo! woe! (> ult. E. ''wellaway'', ''welladay'') = D. ''wee'' = LG. ''wee'' = G. ''weh'' = Icel. ''vei'' = Sw. ''ve'' = Dan. ''vee'' = Goth. ''wai'', interj., woe! (cf. OF. ''ouais'' = It. Sp. ''guai'', woe! < Teut.) = L. ''væ'', woe! (''væ victis'', woe to the vanquished!) = Gr. οἴ! οὐαί! woe! ah! oh! an exclamation of pain, etc., out of which the other uses grew. Hence ult. ''woe'', ''n.'', ''wail''<sup>1</sup>, and ''wellaway'', ''welladay''; cf. also ''waiment.''] Alas! an exclamation of pain or grief. See ''woe'', ''n.''
{{smaller block|Alas and ''woe!'' {{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iv. 14. 107.}}}}

'''woe''' (wō), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Also ''wo''; Sc. ''wae''; < ME. ''wo'', ''woo'', ''wa'', also ''wee'', the last from AS. ''weá'', pl. ''weán'', a form not immediately derivable from the interj. ''wā'', but standing for *''wǣ'' (*''wǣw-'') = OS. ''wē'' (''wēw-'') = D. ''wee'' = LG. ''wee'' = OHG. MHG. ''wē'' (''wēw-''), OHG. also ''wēwo'', m., ''wēwa'', f., G. ''wehe'' = Dan. ''vee'', woe, = Goth. *''wai'' (> It. ''guajo'', pain); prob. from the interj.: see ''woe'', ''interj.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' Grief; sorrow; misery; heavy calamity.
{{smaller block|<poem>They, outcast from God, are here condemn’d
To waste eternal days in ''woe'' and pain.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 695.}}}}
'''2.''' A heavy calamity; an affliction.
{{smaller block|One ''woe'' is past; and, behold, there come two ''woes'' more hereafter. {{right|Rev. ix. 12.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Woe'' is frequently used in denunciations, either with the optative mood of the verb or alone, and thus in an interjectional manner (see ''woe'', ''interj.'').}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|''Woe be'' unto the pastors that destroy and scatter the sheep!
{{right|Jer. xxiii. 1.}}
''Woe'' to the vanquished, ''woe!''
{{right|''Dryden'', Albion and Albanius, i. 1.}}
<poem>''Woe'' to the dupe, and ''woe'' to the deceiver!
''Woe'' to the oppressed, and ''woe'' to the oppressor!</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Hellas.}}}}

{{smaller|It is also used in exclamations of sorrow, in such cases the noun or pronoun following being really in the dative.}}
{{smaller block|''Woe'' is me! for I am undone.
{{right|Isa. vi. 5.}}
''Woe'' was the knight at this severe command.
{{right|''Dryden'', Wife of Bath, l. 108.}}
<poem>An’ aye the o’ercome o’ his sang
Was “''Wae'' ’s me for Prince Charlie!”</poem>
{{right|''W. Glen'', A Wee Bird cam’ to our Ha’ Door.}}}}

{{smaller|'''In weal and woe''', in prosperity and adversity. ''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 987.—'''Woe worth the day.''' See ''worth''<sup>1</sup>, 3.='''Syn.''' Distress, tribulation, affliction, bitterness, unhappiness, wretchedness. ''Woe'' is an intense unhappiness; the word is strong and elevated, almost poetical.}}

'''II.'''† ''a.'' Sad; sorrowful; miserable; woeful; wretched.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ofte hadde Horn beo ''wo''
Ac neure wurs than him was tho.</poem>
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 4.}}
<poem>In this debat I was so ''wo'',
Me thoghte myn herte braste atweyn.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Death of Blanche, l. 1192.}}
He was full ''wo'', and gan his former griefe renew.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. i. 38.}}
<poem>Childe Waters was a ''woe'' man, good Lord,
To see faire Ellen swimme!</poem>
{{right|''Child Waters'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 208).}}}}

'''woebegone, wobegone''' (wō′bẹ̄-gôn″), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. ''woe-begon''; < ME. ''wo-begon'', ''wo-bygon''; < ''woe'', ''wo'', ''n.'', woe, sorrow, + ''begone''<sup>1</sup>.] Overwhelmed with woe; immersed in grief or sor-

readings.c3s3:
row; also, sorrowful; rueful; indicating woe or distress: as, a ''woebegone'' look.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thow farest ek by me, thow Pandarus!
As he that, whan a wight is ''wo-bygon'',
He cometh to him apaas, and seith right thus:
“Thynke nat on smerte and thow shalt fele none!”
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 464.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Coumfort hem that careful been,
And helpe hem that ben ''woo bigoon.''
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 16.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Even such a man, so faint, so spiritless,
So dull, so dead in look, so ''woe-begone'',
Drew Priam’s curtain in the dead of night.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 1. 71.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Each man looked ruefully in his neighbor’s face in search of encouragement, but only found in its ''woe-begone'' lineaments a confirmation of his own dismay.
{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 438.}}}}
{{smaller|In early use the two words are sometimes separated.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wo'' was this wrecched woman tho ''bigoon.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Man of Law’s Tale, l. 820.}}</poem>}}

¶
'''woeful, woful''' (wō′fu̇l), ''a.'' [Sc. ''waeful''; < ME. ''woful, wofull''; < ''woe'' + ''-ful.''] '''1.''' Full of woe; distressed with grief or calamity; afflicted; sorrowful.
{{smaller block|<poem>O verrey goost, that errest to and fro!
Whi niltow flen out of the ''wofulleste''
Body that evere myght on grounde go?
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 303.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>What now willt thou don, ''woful'' Eglentine?
To gret heuynesse off-fors moste thou incline.
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2163.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Weep no more, ''woful'' shepherds.
{{right|''Milton'', Lycidas, l. 165.}}</poem>}}
